# Hover Racer test circuit

Open `project.godot`, then run Main with F6. When reloading external edits, preserve any unsaved editor work separately first. RT / Up applies thrust, LT / Down brakes, left stick makes banked course corrections, right stick / Left and Right arrows rotate the heading more strongly. Y / R resets. With no thrust, turning changes heading while horizontal momentum continues; gas pulls travel toward the new heading.

The circuit is approximately 918 metres long and 28 metres wide, with six rounded corners, a dogleg, and two smooth hills rising 10 and 14 metres. The physical ship follows road collision height; mesh banking, stern swing, powered pitch, and coasting skips do not bob or tilt the camera/collider. FOV changes smoothly by at most 8 degrees. Falling off the course resets the ship.

Ship Inspector tuning: max speed 40 m/s, acceleration 110 m/s², gentle turn speed 0.9 rad/s, strong turn speed 1.8 rad/s, bank 28 degrees, thrust grip 6.5. Throttle is capped at max speed; tapping does not accumulate boosts. Braking remains 20 m/s².

Run checks from this project directory with the Godot executable:

```
Godot --headless --path . --script res://tests/controller_input.gd
Godot --headless --path . --script res://tests/hill_course.gd
```

These simulate controls, inspect road/barrier collisions, and traverse both hills at maximum speed. They cannot judge physical controller feel or visual comfort. The hill test uses the default tuning above.

`tools/build_course.gd` generates the saved TestTrack scene and `tools/course_samples.json`; running it replaces TestTrack.tscn, so back up any manual course edits first. Pre-update files are in `backups/before-speed-hills` (project settings backup has a `.bak` suffix).
